Implementation (`catalog/catalog.go:37`):

```go
resolved := make(map[string]string)
asked := make(map[string]struct{}, len(skus))
for _, sku := range skus {
	if _, seen := asked[sku]; seen {
		continue
	}
	asked[sku] = struct{}{}
	name, err := src.Get(sku)
	if errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound) {
		continue
	}
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("catalog: resolve %q: %w", sku, err)
	}
	resolved[sku] = name
}
return resolved, nil
```

`asked` is tracked separately from `resolved` because an unknown SKU is absent from the result yet must still not be asked for twice. `errors.Is` (not `err == ErrNotFound`) so a Source that wraps the sentinel is still treated as unknown, and `%w` so the caller can reach `ErrClosed` and transport causes with `errors.Is`, while the text names the SKU for the operator's log line.

`catalog/catalog_contract_test.go` holds one case per clause: order, unknown-is-skipped, repeat-costs-one for both a known and an unknown SKU (the unknown one is where a result-keyed cache leaks), abandoning the walk on transport and on `ErrClosed` with the recorded call list proving the later SKU was never requested, error text and `errors.Is` reachability including a wrapped cause, empty/nil input making no round trips, and a separate test that a wrapped `ErrNotFound` never reaches the caller.

Resolve looks up each SKU at most once, skipping unknowns and returning a SKU-naming wrapped error on any other failure.
